#set document(
  title: "Lists — bullet and numbered markers with hanging indent",
  author: "Mosaic contributors",
  language: "en",
)

#set page(
  paper: "A4",
  margin: 24mm,
)

= Lists

Mosaic recognises two list flavours at the block level. An unordered
list is opened by a line starting with `- `; the marker renders as a
`•` in the gutter to the left of the item's text, and continuation
across line wraps keeps the same hanging indent:

- A short item.
- A longer item that intentionally runs past the right margin so the
  greedy line breaker wraps it onto a second line — the wrapped text
  hangs flush with the first line's left edge, never sliding back
  under the marker.
- *Italic*, **bold**, and `inline code` flow through unchanged inside
  an item.

== Nesting

Indenting a marker line by two spaces makes it a child of the most
recent item at the outer indent. Indents nest arbitrarily deep, and
ordered / unordered lists can mix freely across levels:

- Outer item one.
  - First nested child.
  - Second nested child, with *emphasis*.
- Outer item two.
  - Mixing marker kinds at a deeper level is fine:
    1. ordered grandchild,
    2. and another.
- Outer item three.

== Numbered lists renumber from 1

The digits typed in the source are ignored at render time — the layout
engine numbers each item starting from 1, in source order:

5. first step,
9. second step,
27. and a long third step that runs well past the right margin so the
line breaker has to wrap it back onto a second line at the same
indent as the first — proving the hanging indent survives across
wraps in numbered lists too.

== Wide markers

The gutter sizes itself against the widest marker the list will emit,
so long ordered runs don't crash their numbers into the text column:

1. one,
2. two,
3. three,
4. four,
5. five,
6. six,
7. seven,
8. eight,
9. nine,
10. ten,
11. eleven,
12. twelve.
